  "details": "Recently i've been discussing various physics models that are stable, visually correct, and consistent for networked game scenarios, and been running into stuff like this\n\n![](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7hK1g8CvbgY)\n\nhttps://github.com/Kimbatt/unity-deterministic-physics\n\nas an example of an asset for this and accompanying video, which solves cross-platform using software floats *(if there are issues with soft floats or not wanting to have to convert everything, have a networked buffer between regular floats as surrogates for interacting with the softfloat physics space)*\n\nhttps://forum.unity.com/threads/burst-compiler-deterministic-floats.522856/#post-7093750 unity thread relating to the topic, that also mentions the same above system\n\n...there is also the time tested well known system used in engines like source (half life, gmod etc) and blam (halo), Havok physics...\n\nhttps://docs.unity3d.com/Packages/com.havok.physics@1.2/manual/index.html\n\nwhich should only have issues being consistent to mobile hardware, but interpolating only for mobile only should be perfectly acceptable due to their already inconsistent experience with pc anyway.\n\n---\n\nUpon extensive discussion and consideration, as well as comparing to many peoples' user experiences throughout the years, it's kind of come to a clear realization that unsynchronized or parented and interpolated physics motion in vrchat is absolutely horrendous and damages the interactive experience quite a lot across the board.  Being able to have props and objects, collisions, ragdolls etc deterministically processed on each user's end would by a pretty large factor in solidifying interactive immersion inside vrchat -- this is especially so due to the nature of vr being a kinetic experience. \n\nThe \"catch up\" interpolated position of objects in worlds, as well as dsynchronized rigidbody behaviours etc have made things extremely limiting, and have forced people to try and engineer malperformant udon-based physics systems which are not widely applicable.\n\n---\n\nOverall the point to be gotten here is one referencing the massive success and entertainment value gained from physics sandboxing (especially when referencing GMod and Halo, which became of the most popular sandbox games of all time, and the most popular physics sandbox games), which could only get significantly better in the context of social vr.",
